pj("V01-F-001", "Society and culture", "The queue is enforced only by the attention and disapproval of those standing in it.", "Easy",
   ["Few social institutions are as quietly enforced as the queue, which works without officials, written rules or any formal penalty for breaking it.",
    "Its authority rests instead on the watchful attention of the people standing in it, each of whom notices at once when someone tries to slip ahead.",
    "That collective vigilance explains why a queue-jumper is usually met not with force but with stares, pointed remarks and audible sighs.",
    "Such small signals of disapproval are often enough, because most people find public embarrassment more costly than a few minutes of waiting."],
   "Sentence [[1]] introduces the queue and says it has no formal enforcement. Sentence [[2]] says its authority rests 'instead' on the attention of the people in it, which answers the absence named in [[1]]. Sentence [[3]] refers back to 'That collective vigilance', the attention described in [[2]], and names the stares and remarks it produces. Sentence [[4]] opens with 'Such small signals of disapproval', which can only mean the stares and sighs of [[3]], and explains why they work.",
   "Putting sentence [[4]] before sentence [[3]] because both describe disapproval, which leaves 'Such small signals' with nothing to refer to.",
   "V01F-queueing-norms", "Each link is marked by a reference phrase ('instead', 'That collective vigilance', 'Such small signals'), so the order can be read off quickly.",
   traps=[("1243", "Sentence [[4]] cannot follow sentence [[2]]: 'Such small signals of disapproval' needs the stares and sighs that only sentence [[3]] mentions.")],
   cognitive="Understanding")

pj("V01-F-002", "History and politics", "Longitude at sea depended on knowing home-port time, which needed clocks steady enough to keep it.", "Easy",
   ["For centuries, sailors out of sight of land could find their latitude from the height of the sun, but their longitude remained a matter of guesswork.",
    "The difficulty was that longitude is, in effect, a difference in time, since the earth turns through fifteen degrees of longitude every hour.",
    "A navigator who knew the time at his home port could compare it with local noon at sea and work out how far east or west he had sailed.",
    "The ordinary clocks of the day, however, lost or gained minutes on a rolling ship, and every minute of error meant miles of uncertainty.",
    "The problem was largely solved in the eighteenth century, when clockmakers built marine timekeepers steady enough to carry home-port time across an ocean."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states the problem: longitude was guesswork. Sentence [[2]] explains 'The difficulty', that longitude is a difference in time. Sentence [[3]] shows how a navigator could use that fact if he knew home-port time. Sentence [[4]] says 'however' that clocks of the day could not keep that time at sea, which blocks the method of [[3]]. Sentence [[5]] says how 'The problem' was finally solved, with clocks steady enough to do what [[4]] says ordinary clocks could not.",
   "Placing sentence [[4]] before sentence [[3]] because both mention time at sea, although 'however' in sentence [[4]] objects to the method of comparing clocks, which only sentence [[3]] has described.",
   "V01F-longitude-at-sea", "A problem, its explanation, a method, the obstacle to the method and the solution follow in sequence, with 'The difficulty' and 'however' as clear markers.",
   traps=[("13245", "Sentence [[3]] relies on the idea that longitude is a difference in time, which sentence [[2]] introduces; and 'The difficulty' in sentence [[2]] must directly follow the guesswork of sentence [[1]].")],
   cognitive="Understanding")

pj("V01-F-003", "Science and environment", "Coral bleaching is the loss of symbiotic algae under heat stress, which can starve the coral.", "Easy",
   ["Reef-building corals depend on microscopic algae that live inside their tissues and supply much of the energy the corals need to grow.",
    "When sea temperatures stay unusually high for weeks, this partnership breaks down and the corals expel the algae they normally shelter.",
    "Without them the coral tissue becomes nearly transparent, so the pale skeleton beneath shows through, which is why the event is called bleaching.",
    "A bleached coral is not yet dead, but unless cooler water allows the algae to return, it may starve within a matter of months."],
   "Sentence [[1]] introduces the dependence of corals on algae. Sentence [[2]] calls this 'this partnership' and describes how heat breaks it. Sentence [[3]] begins 'Without them', meaning without the algae just expelled in [[2]], and explains the name 'bleaching'. Sentence [[4]] picks up 'A bleached coral', a term defined only in [[3]], and states the consequence.",
   "Putting sentence [[4]] before sentence [[3]], although 'bleached' is explained only in sentence [[3]].",
   "V01F-coral-bleaching", "Cause, effect and consequence are signalled by 'this partnership', 'Without them' and the defined term 'bleached'.",
   traps=[("1243", "Sentence [[4]] uses 'A bleached coral' before the term is introduced, and sentence [[3]] would then begin 'Without them' after a sentence about starvation rather than about the expelled algae.")],
   cognitive="Understanding")

pj("V01-F-004", "Technology", "Barcodes removed manual price entry and also gave shops instant sales records.", "Easy",
   ["Before barcodes, a supermarket cashier had to read the price of every item and key it into the till by hand.",
    "This was slow, and each keystroke was a chance for error, whether an honest slip or a deliberate favour to a friend.",
    "The barcode replaced that step with a scanner that reads a printed pattern of lines and looks up the price in a central record.",
    "Because the same record also tracks what has been sold, the shop learns at once which shelves need restocking."],
   "Sentence [[1]] describes manual price entry. Sentence [[2]] begins 'This was slow', judging the practice of [[1]]. Sentence [[3]] says the barcode 'replaced that step', the keying-in of prices, and introduces the 'central record'. Sentence [[4]] refers to 'the same record' and adds a second benefit.",
   "Placing sentence [[3]] straight after sentence [[1]] and sentence [[2]] after it, which would make 'This was slow' describe the scanner.",
   "V01F-barcode-checkout", "Reference words ('This', 'that step', 'the same record') carry each link.",
   traps=[("1324", "With sentence [[2]] after sentence [[3]], 'This was slow' would describe the scanner, the opposite of what is meant, and sentence [[4]] would lose its link to 'a central record' in sentence [[3]].")],
   cognitive="Understanding")

pj("V01-F-005", "Society and culture", "If children find other status markers, uniforms shift rather than end competition over appearance.", "Easy",
   ["Supporters of school uniforms often argue that they reduce the pressure on children to compete through expensive clothing.",
    "When everyone wears the same blazer, the reasoning goes, a child from a poorer family is less likely to be singled out.",
    "Critics reply that children simply find other ways to display status, through shoes, bags, phones or haircuts.",
    "If the critics are right, uniforms may shift the competition rather than end it, so the case for them would have to rest on something other than equality."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states the supporters' argument. Sentence [[2]] spells it out ('the reasoning goes'). Sentence [[3]] gives the critics' reply. Sentence [[4]] begins 'If the critics are right' and draws the consequence, so it must follow [[3]].",
   "Placing sentence [[3]] before sentence [[2]], which leaves 'the reasoning goes' explaining the critics' view rather than the supporters'.",
   "V01F-school-uniforms", "A familiar argument, reply and consequence structure with explicit markers.",
   traps=[("1324", "Sentence [[2]] spells out the supporters' reasoning, so it belongs beside sentence [[1]]; after the critics' reply it would confuse whose reasoning is meant.")],
   cognitive="Understanding")

pj("V01-F-006", "Psychology", "Habits are triggered by cues, so changing surroundings helps to break them.", "Easy",
   ["Many habits are triggered less by conscious decisions than by cues in our surroundings, such as a time of day, a place or an object in view.",
    "A person who always smokes with morning coffee, for example, may find the craving arrives with the cup rather than with any thought of smoking.",
    "This is why people trying to break a habit are often advised to change their surroundings as well as their intentions.",
    "Such a change works because it removes the cue, taking away the prompt that would otherwise set the old behaviour in motion."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states that cues trigger habits. Sentence [[2]] gives an example ('for example'). Sentence [[3]] draws the practical advice with 'This is why'. Sentence [[4]] refers to 'Such a change', the change of surroundings advised in [[3]], and explains why it works.",
   "Placing sentence [[4]] before sentence [[3]] as an explanation of the coffee example, although 'Such a change' refers to the advice to change one's surroundings, given only in sentence [[3]].",
   "V01F-habit-cues", "Claim, example, advice and explanation, each tied to the previous sentence by a marker.",
   traps=[("1243", "Sentence [[4]] speaks of 'Such a change' before any change has been mentioned; the change of surroundings first appears in sentence [[3]].")],
   cognitive="Understanding")

pj("V01-F-007", "Science and environment", "The ozone layer is recovering slowly after damaging chemicals were phased out.", "Easy",
   ["High in the atmosphere, a thin layer of ozone absorbs much of the sun's ultraviolet radiation before it can reach the ground.",
    "In the twentieth century, chemicals widely used in refrigerators and aerosol sprays drifted upwards and began to break this ozone apart.",
    "The damage became impossible to ignore when scientists in the 1980s reported a deep seasonal thinning of the layer over Antarctica.",
    "Governments responded with an international agreement to phase out the offending chemicals, and their production has since fallen to a small fraction of its peak.",
    "Even so, the layer is recovering only slowly, because the chemicals already released can survive in the atmosphere for decades."],
   "Sentence [[1]] introduces the ozone layer. Sentence [[2]] refers to 'this ozone' and describes the chemicals that attacked it. Sentence [[3]] says 'The damage' was recognised in the 1980s. Sentence [[4]] gives the governments' response to that recognition. Sentence [[5]] qualifies the response with 'Even so': production fell, yet recovery is slow.",
   "Placing sentence [[5]] before sentence [[4]], although 'Even so' concedes something, the fall in production, that only sentence [[4]] reports.",
   "V01F-ozone-recovery", "A chronological chain marked by 'this ozone', 'The damage', 'responded' and 'Even so'.",
   traps=[("12354", "Sentence [[5]]'s 'Even so' needs the success of the phase-out in sentence [[4]] before it; placed earlier it qualifies nothing."),
          ("13245", "Sentence [[3]]'s 'The damage' has no referent unless sentence [[2]] has described the chemicals breaking the ozone apart.")],
   cognitive="Understanding")

pj("V01-F-008", "Society and culture", "Nineteenth-century urban parks were designed as space shared across classes.", "Easy",
   ["Many of the large public parks in industrial cities were laid out in the nineteenth century, when rapid growth had packed workers into crowded and smoky streets.",
    "Reformers of the time argued that such conditions harmed both health and morals, and that open green space would act as the lungs of the city.",
    "The parks they campaigned for were meant to be open to everyone, so that a factory hand and a merchant might walk the same paths.",
    "That ideal of a space shared across classes was as much a part of the design as the lawns and trees themselves."],
   "Sentence [[1]] sets the scene of crowded industrial cities. Sentence [[2]] refers to 'such conditions' and gives the reformers' argument. Sentence [[3]] describes 'The parks they campaigned for', where 'they' are the reformers of [[2]]. Sentence [[4]] names 'That ideal' of shared space, which [[3]] has just described.",
   "Placing sentence [[3]] before sentence [[2]], which leaves 'they' in sentence [[3]] without the reformers to refer to.",
   "V01F-public-parks", "The links 'such conditions', 'they' and 'That ideal' make the order straightforward.",
   traps=[("1324", "Sentence [[3]] says 'The parks they campaigned for', but the campaigners are introduced only in sentence [[2]]; sentence [[2]]'s 'such conditions' also needs to follow the crowded streets of sentence [[1]].")],
   cognitive="Understanding")

pj("V01-F-009", "Psychology", "Hindsight bias makes past decisions look worse than they were, so fair judgement must ask what was knowable then.", "Medium",
   ["Once we know how an event turned out, it becomes remarkably hard to remember how uncertain it looked beforehand.",
    "Asked afterwards, people tend to report that they had expected the outcome all along, even when their earlier recorded forecasts said otherwise.",
    "This tendency matters most when we judge others, since a decision that was reasonable on the information available can look careless in the light of what followed.",
    "Such judgements, whether of a doctor's diagnosis or an investor's choice, must therefore ask what could have been known at the time, not what we know now."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states the general phenomenon. Sentence [[2]] gives the evidence: people asked 'afterwards' claim they expected the outcome. Sentence [[3]] names 'This tendency' and turns to its importance when we judge others. Sentence [[4]] begins 'Such judgements', referring to the judging of others introduced in [[3]], applies the point to a doctor and an investor and draws the conclusion with 'therefore'.",
   "Placing sentence [[3]] directly after sentence [[1]] because 'This tendency' seems to fit the general statement, then ending with sentence [[2]], which strands the evidence after the argument has moved on to judging others.",
   "V01F-hindsight-bias", "Two orders look possible at first because 'This tendency' could refer to the general phenomenon or to the evidence of self-report; the reader must see that the concluding advice about judging a doctor or investor follows only from the point about judging others.",
   traps=[("1243", "Sentence [[4]] begins 'Such judgements', which needs the judging of others introduced in sentence [[3]]; after sentence [[2]] it has nothing to refer to."),
          ("1324", "With sentence [[2]] after sentence [[3]], the paragraph moves to judging others and then drops back to self-report, and the 'therefore' of sentence [[4]] no longer follows from the point about judging others."),
          ("2134", "Sentence [[2]] cannot open: 'Asked afterwards' needs the event and its outcome introduced in sentence [[1]].")])

pj("V01-F-010", "Psychology", "Witnesses help less in crowds because responsibility is diffused and hesitation is contagious.", "Medium",
   ["It seems natural to assume that a person in distress is more likely to receive help when many people are nearby to see it.",
    "A long line of psychological research has suggested the opposite: the more witnesses there are, the less likely any single one of them is to step forward.",
    "Part of the explanation is that responsibility feels divided, so that each witness assumes someone else is better placed to act.",
    "Another part is that people look to one another for cues, and a crowd of hesitant faces makes the situation appear less serious than it is.",
    "Both pressures weaken when one witness is singled out, which is why first-aid trainers advise pointing at a specific person and asking that person to call for help."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states the natural assumption. Sentence [[2]] reports 'the opposite' finding, which needs the assumption before it. Sentence [[3]] gives 'Part of the explanation' and sentence [[4]] 'Another part', so [[3]] precedes [[4]]. Sentence [[5]] refers to 'Both pressures', the two explanations, and draws the practical lesson.",
   "Opening with sentence [[2]] because it states the striking finding, although 'the opposite' requires the assumption in sentence [[1]].",
   "V01F-bystander-effect", "The assumption and reversal must be paired correctly, and the two explanations ordered by 'Part' and 'Another part' before 'Both pressures'.",
   traps=[("21345", "Sentence [[2]] cannot open because 'the opposite' needs the assumption that crowds bring help, stated in sentence [[1]]."),
          ("12435", "Sentence [[4]] begins 'Another part', which presupposes the first part given in sentence [[3]].")])

pj("V01-F-011", "History and politics", "Medieval guilds were cartels but also guaranteed quality to buyers who could not judge it.", "Medium",
   ["Medieval craft guilds are often remembered as cartels that kept out competitors and held prices high for the benefit of their members.",
    "That picture is not wrong, but it leaves out the services guilds provided to buyers who had no easy way to judge quality for themselves.",
    "By inspecting workshops and punishing members who sold shoddy goods, a guild put its collective reputation behind every item its members made.",
    "A customer buying cloth from a stranger in a distant town could therefore rely on a guild's mark where he could not rely on the seller's word."],
   "Sentence [[1]] gives the common view of guilds as cartels. Sentence [[2]] calls it 'That picture' and points to the overlooked service to buyers. Sentence [[3]] explains how the service worked: inspection made the guild's reputation stand behind its goods. Sentence [[4]] draws the consequence with 'therefore': the customer can trust the guild's mark, which follows from the reputation described in [[3]].",
   "Placing sentence [[4]] before sentence [[3]] because the customer example seems to illustrate sentence [[2]], although its 'therefore' depends on the guild's reputation, which only sentence [[3]] establishes.",
   "V01F-medieval-guilds", "The last two sentences must be ordered by reasoning: the mechanism of inspection must come before the buyer's reliance that it justifies.",
   traps=[("1243", "Sentence [[4]] says the customer could 'therefore' rely on a guild's mark, but the reason for that reliance, collective reputation backed by inspection, appears only in sentence [[3]].")])

pj("V01-F-012", "History and politics", "Metric units appealed because, belonging to no locality, they could be accepted by all.", "Medium",
   ["Before the metric system, a unit such as the foot or the pound might differ from one town to the next, and sometimes from one trade to another.",
    "The variety was not mere muddle, since local measures were often tied to local customs, such as the area a team could plough in a day.",
    "For merchants and tax officials, however, that same variety was a constant source of disputes, because the same word could name different quantities in different markets.",
    "Reformers in late eighteenth-century France proposed a remedy: units derived from nature rather than custom, linked to one another by powers of ten.",
    "The appeal of the scheme lay in that very detachment, for a measure that belonged to no locality could be accepted by all of them."],
   "Sentence [[1]] describes the variety of units. Sentence [[2]] names 'The variety' and concedes that it had a basis in local custom. Sentence [[3]] turns with 'however': for merchants and officials 'that same variety', named in [[2]], caused disputes. Sentence [[4]] proposes 'a remedy' for those disputes, units based on nature rather than custom. Sentence [[5]] refers to 'that very detachment', the break from custom in [[4]], and explains its appeal.",
   "Placing sentence [[3]] straight after sentence [[1]] as the obvious complaint, then sentence [[2]] as a concession, which leaves 'however' in sentence [[3]] with no defence of the units to answer and puts the defence of custom just before the remedy it is meant to motivate.",
   "V01F-metric-system", "The concession and reply must be ordered by the logic of the argument: the defence of local custom comes first, the commercial objection answers it, and the remedy answers the objection.",
   traps=[("13245", "Sentence [[3]] turns against a positive view with 'however', and 'that same variety' needs 'The variety' named in sentence [[2]]; after sentence [[1]] there is no favourable view to contrast with, and sentence [[2]] would then interrupt between the problem and its remedy."),
          ("12354", "Sentence [[5]]'s 'that very detachment' refers to units derived 'from nature rather than custom', which only sentence [[4]] introduces.")])

pj("V01-F-013", "Economics and business", "Shared pastures are overgrazed because each user gains fully but bears only part of the cost.", "Medium",
   ["Consider a pasture open to every herder in a village, where each additional animal brings its owner the full gain from its grazing.",
    "The cost of that animal, a slight thinning of the grass, is spread across everyone who uses the pasture.",
    "Each herder thus has reason to add animals even when the pasture as a whole is already strained, and so does every other herder.",
    "The result is a field grazed bare by people who each acted sensibly, which is why shared resources often need rules that limit what each user may take."],
   "Sentence [[1]] sets up the hypothetical pasture and the private gain from each animal. Sentence [[2]] gives the other half of the calculation, the shared cost of 'that animal'. Sentence [[3]] combines the two with 'thus': gain is private and cost is shared, so each herder keeps adding animals. Sentence [[4]] states 'The result' and the lesson. The argument needs both the gain and the cost before the conclusion in [[3]].",
   "Placing sentence [[3]] straight after sentence [[1]], although the herder's reason to add animals 'even when the pasture as a whole is already strained' depends on the cost being shared, which only sentence [[2]] states.",
   "V01F-tragedy-of-commons", "The order follows the logic of the argument: private gain, shared cost, the incentive that follows from both, then the outcome.",
   traps=[("1324", "Sentence [[3]] draws its 'thus' from the contrast between full private gain and spread cost; without sentence [[2]] the reason to overload a strained pasture is missing, and 'that animal' in sentence [[2]] would then follow a sentence about many animals.")])

pj("V01-F-014", "Economics and business", "Rent control protects sitting tenants but can disadvantage newcomers by discouraging supply.", "Medium",
   ["Rent control appeals to a simple sense of fairness: tenants should not be priced out of homes they already occupy.",
    "For those who hold a controlled lease, the policy often delivers exactly that, and they may stay put for decades at a modest rent.",
    "The difficulty lies with the people who do not yet have such a lease, since landlords facing capped returns have less reason to build, maintain or let new flats.",
    "A city may thus end up protecting its current tenants at the expense of newcomers, who face fewer and costlier homes to choose from."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states the fairness argument. Sentence [[2]] says the policy 'delivers exactly that' for current leaseholders. Sentence [[3]] turns to people 'who do not yet have such a lease' and the effect on supply. Sentence [[4]] draws the conclusion with 'thus', contrasting current tenants with newcomers, which needs both [[2]] and [[3]].",
   "Ending with sentence [[3]] after sentence [[4]], although the newcomers' shortage of homes in sentence [[4]] follows from the reduced supply described in sentence [[3]].",
   "V01F-rent-control", "The last three sentences must be ordered by the logic of winners, losers and the trade-off between them.",
   traps=[("1243", "Sentence [[4]]'s 'thus' requires the reason newcomers lose, the reduced supply of flats, which only sentence [[3]] gives.")])

pj("V01-F-015", "Science and environment", "Earthworms improve soil by burrowing, pulling down leaves and leaving rich casts.", "Medium",
   ["Earthworms are easily dismissed as humble creatures, yet few animals do more to shape the soil in which plants grow.",
    "As they burrow, they open channels through which air and rainwater can reach the roots below.",
    "They also drag fallen leaves down into their tunnels, carrying dead plant matter from the surface into the soil where it can decay.",
    "The leaves they swallow, along with the soil around them, pass through their bodies and emerge as casts that are richer in nutrients than the surrounding earth.",
    "Over many years, this ceaseless digging, feeding and excreting can turn over the whole upper layer of a field, which is why farmers have long welcomed worms."],
   "Sentence [[1]] introduces the claim that worms shape the soil. Sentence [[2]] gives the first effect, burrowing. Sentence [[3]] adds a second ('also'): pulling leaves into the tunnels. Sentence [[4]] follows those leaves through the worm's body ('The leaves they swallow'), so it must come after [[3]]. Sentence [[5]] sums up the 'digging, feeding and excreting' of [[2]], [[3]] and [[4]] in that order and concludes.",
   "Placing sentence [[4]] before sentence [[3]] because casts seem a separate effect, although 'The leaves they swallow' presupposes the leaves dragged underground in sentence [[3]].",
   "V01F-earthworms-soil", "The three effects look like a list that could be reordered; the reader must see that the leaves dragged underground are what pass through the worm to form casts, and that 'also' places leaf-dragging after burrowing.",
   traps=[("12435", "Sentence [[4]] speaks of 'The leaves they swallow' before any leaves have been mentioned; they enter the soil in sentence [[3]]."),
          ("13245", "Sentence [[3]] says the worms 'also' drag leaves down, which presupposes the first activity, burrowing, in sentence [[2]]; and 'their tunnels' needs the burrows of sentence [[2]].")])

pj("V01-F-016", "Technology", "Batteries let weather-dependent power match demand on a grid that must balance supply instantly.", "Medium",
   ["Electricity is unusual among goods in that, on a conventional grid, it must be produced at the very moment it is consumed.",
    "Power stations that burn fuel can meet this requirement by raising or lowering output as demand changes through the day.",
    "Solar panels and wind turbines cannot be steered in the same way, since their output follows the weather rather than the needs of consumers.",
    "Large batteries help to close this gap by storing surplus power when the sun shines or the wind blows and releasing it later."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states the requirement that supply match demand instantly. Sentence [[2]] calls it 'this requirement' and shows how fuel-burning stations meet it. Sentence [[3]] contrasts solar and wind, which 'cannot be steered in the same way' as the stations of [[2]]. Sentence [[4]] refers to 'this gap', the mismatch between weather-driven output and demand, and gives the remedy.",
   "Placing sentence [[3]] before sentence [[2]] because both concern power sources, although 'in the same way' needs the adjustable stations of sentence [[2]].",
   "V01F-grid-battery-storage", "The order follows the problem: the requirement, a source that meets it, a source that does not, and the remedy for the mismatch.",
   traps=[("1324", "Sentence [[3]] says solar and wind cannot be steered 'in the same way', which presupposes the adjustable stations of sentence [[2]]; sentence [[4]] would also follow a sentence about fuel stations, which create no gap.")])

pj("V01-F-017", "Technology", "Standard containers cut handling, and standardisation let all transport be built around them.", "Medium",
   ["Until the middle of the twentieth century, cargo was loaded onto ships piece by piece, with dockworkers carrying sacks, barrels and crates by hand or on slings.",
    "Loading a single vessel in this way could take days, and goods were often damaged or stolen while they sat on the quay.",
    "Standard steel containers changed the job entirely, since a sealed box could be lifted by crane from a lorry to a ship and back without its contents being touched.",
    "Because the boxes were all the same size, ports, ships, trains and lorries could be built around them, and the cost of moving goods across the world fell sharply."],
   "Sentence [[1]] describes piece-by-piece loading. Sentence [[2]] says loading 'in this way' was slow and risky. Sentence [[3]] introduces containers, which answer both problems of [[2]]: sealed boxes are moved by crane and untouched. Sentence [[4]] refers to 'the boxes' and adds the further effect of standard size.",
   "Placing sentence [[4]] before sentence [[3]], although 'the boxes' in sentence [[4]] are introduced as containers only in sentence [[3]].",
   "V01F-shipping-containers", "The reader must see that the container sentence answers both problems of manual loading (delay and theft) and that the effect of standard size builds on the containers already introduced.",
   traps=[("1324", "Sentence [[2]]'s 'in this way' refers to manual loading, so it must follow sentence [[1]]; after sentence [[3]] it would describe container loading as slow, the opposite of the point.")])

pj("V01-F-018", "Technology", "Dark patterns steer users by making the company's preferred option the easiest, which is what makes them troubling.", "Medium",
   ["Designers of websites and apps have learned that small choices about layout can steer users without their noticing.",
    "A subscription that takes one click to start but several screens to cancel, or a box pre-ticked to accept marketing emails, works this way.",
    "Such designs, often called dark patterns, do not forbid anything, since the subscriber can still cancel and the box can still be unticked; they simply make the option the company prefers the easiest one to take.",
    "Critics argue that this is precisely what makes them troubling, since a user who never noticed being steered has no reason to object."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states that layout can steer users unnoticed. Sentence [[2]] gives examples that work 'this way'. Sentence [[3]] names 'Such designs' as dark patterns and, referring to 'the subscriber' and 'the box' of the examples in [[2]], says they forbid nothing. Sentence [[4]] says 'this is precisely' what makes them troubling, referring to the quiet steering of [[3]], and links back to the unnoticed steering of [[1]].",
   "Starting with sentence [[3]] because it names the subject, although 'Such designs' requires the examples in sentence [[2]].",
   "V01F-dark-patterns", "Students must see that the examples come before the naming of the category, and that the critics' point refers to the description of how dark patterns work.",
   traps=[("3124", "Sentence [[3]] cannot open: 'Such designs' needs the examples of sentence [[2]] before it."),
          ("1324", "Sentence [[3]] speaks of 'the subscriber' who can still cancel and 'the box' that can still be unticked, which are introduced only in the examples of sentence [[2]]; and sentence [[4]]'s 'this' would then point at the examples rather than at the steering without prohibition.")])

pj("V01-F-019", "Society and culture", "Gossip enforces good conduct as an informal reputation system, at the cost of spreading falsehoods.", "Medium",
   ["Gossip has a poor reputation, yet in small communities it has long done work that no formal institution could do as cheaply.",
    "Talk about who failed to repay a loan or broke a promise spreads knowledge of people's conduct far beyond those who witnessed it.",
    "Since any lapse may become common knowledge in this way, most people have a strong motive to behave well even when no one is watching at the moment.",
    "Gossip thus acts as an informal system of reputation, though the same mechanism that punishes cheats can also spread falsehoods about the innocent."],
   "Sentence [[1]] claims gossip does useful work. Sentence [[2]] shows what that work is: talk spreads knowledge of conduct. Sentence [[3]] draws the effect 'in this way': lapses become known, so people behave well. Sentence [[4]] concludes with 'thus' that gossip is a reputation system and adds a qualification.",
   "Placing sentence [[3]] before sentence [[2]] because the motive to behave well seems to follow from gossip in general, although 'in this way' refers to the spreading of knowledge described in sentence [[2]].",
   "V01F-gossip-function", "The reader must follow the mechanism (talk spreads knowledge, so lapses are costly, so gossip works as reputation) rather than rely on topic words.",
   traps=[("1324", "Sentence [[3]] says lapses become known 'in this way', but the way, talk spreading knowledge beyond witnesses, is described only in sentence [[2]].")])

pj("V01-F-020", "Psychology", "Beliefs direct the search for confirming cases, so a real test asks what would be seen if the belief were false.", "Difficult",
   ["When people test a belief, they tend to look for cases that would fit it rather than cases that would refute it.",
    "Someone convinced, say, that a colleague is lazy will notice each late arrival and pass over the many mornings when the colleague came early.",
    "Each late arrival then feels like fresh proof of laziness, although it was noticed only because the belief directed where to look.",
    "The remedy is not to have fewer beliefs but to ask, deliberately, what one would expect to see if the belief were false.",
    "Evidence gathered in answer to that question can genuinely test a belief, because it was not selected by the belief it is meant to judge."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states the tendency. Sentence [[2]] illustrates it ('say') with the colleague. Sentence [[3]] picks up 'Each late arrival' from [[2]] and explains why such cases feel like proof though the belief chose them. Sentence [[4]] offers the remedy, and sentence [[5]] explains why the remedy works, referring to 'that question' asked in [[4]]. The closing reason in [[5]], evidence 'not selected by the belief', answers exactly the flaw identified in [[3]].",
   "Opening with the concrete case of the colleague, or treating sentence [[5]] as a general claim that could come before the remedy, although 'that question' needs the question posed in sentence [[4]].",
   "V01F-confirmation-bias", "Difficult because the paragraph turns on the idea that the belief itself selects its evidence; the reader must see that the remedy and its justification answer that flaw, and resist the tempting example-first opening.",
   traps=[("21345", "Sentence [[2]] contains 'say', which marks it as an illustration of a claim already made; it cannot open before the general tendency in sentence [[1]]."),
          ("12354", "Sentence [[5]] refers to 'that question', which is the question proposed in sentence [[4]]; before it, there is no question to answer."),
          ("14523", "Offering the remedy before the example leaves the illustration marked by 'say' far from the tendency it illustrates, and the paragraph would end on the flaw after its cure had already been given.")],
   exams=HARD)

pj("V01-F-021", "Economics and business", "Whether a minimum wage cuts jobs depends on whether local employers can set wages.", "Difficult",
   ["The simplest textbook model predicts that a minimum wage set above the market rate must reduce employment, since labour has become more expensive.",
    "Yet that prediction assumes employers have no power to set wages, competing for workers so fiercely that each is paid exactly what the market will bear.",
    "Where a few employers dominate a local labour market, they have exactly such power, and a modest minimum may then draw more people into work rather than fewer.",
    "The debate is thus less about whether the textbook logic is valid than about which kind of labour market a particular town actually resembles."],
   "Sentence [[1]] gives the textbook prediction. Sentence [[2]] identifies the assumption behind 'that prediction', that employers cannot set wages. Sentence [[3]] describes a market where that assumption fails: dominant employers have 'exactly such power', the power to set wages denied in [[2]]. Sentence [[4]] concludes with 'thus' that the question is which kind of market a town is, a conclusion that needs both the textbook case and the exception.",
   "Treating sentence [[3]] as an immediate counterexample to sentence [[1]] and placing sentence [[2]] after it, although 'such power' in sentence [[3]] refers to the wage-setting power named only in sentence [[2]].",
   "V01F-minimum-wage", "The economic reasoning (prediction, hidden assumption, case where the assumption fails, reframed debate) is abstract, and the sentence about dominant employers looks at first like a direct reply to the textbook prediction.",
   traps=[("1324", "Sentence [[3]] says dominant employers have 'exactly such power', but no power has been mentioned until sentence [[2]]; and sentence [[2]]'s 'Yet' would then contrast with a sentence that already makes its point.")],
   exams=HARD)

pj("V01-F-022", "Economics and business", "Specialisation pays only where the market is large enough, so it grows with trade.", "Difficult",
   ["Dividing a task among specialists raises output, because each worker becomes quicker at a narrow job than anyone could be at the whole.",
    "But the gain depends on the size of the market, since a specialist is worth employing only if there is enough demand to keep him busy at his one task.",
    "A village blacksmith must therefore do every kind of metalwork himself, while a large city can support separate makers of nails, locks and hinges.",
    "Specialisation is thus less a choice that any workshop can make than a reward that grows as trade widens the circle of buyers."],
   "Sentence [[1]] states the benefit of dividing work. Sentence [[2]] qualifies it with 'But the gain depends on the size of the market'. Sentence [[3]] applies that condition ('therefore') to the village and the city: the small market cannot keep specialists busy, the large one can. Sentence [[4]] concludes with 'thus' that specialisation depends on the extent of trade.",
   "Reading the blacksmith of sentence [[3]] as an illustration of sentence [[1]], although the village smith is the opposite of a specialist and his case follows only from the market-size condition in sentence [[2]].",
   "V01F-division-of-labour", "Difficult because the economic reasoning is abstract and the example of the village and the city can look like an illustration of specialisation in general rather than of its dependence on market size.",
   traps=[("1324", "Sentence [[3]]'s 'therefore' needs a reason why the village smith cannot specialise; sentence [[1]] gives only the benefits of specialisation, and it is the market-size condition in sentence [[2]] that explains the village case.")],
   exams=HARD)

pj("V01-F-023", "Economics and business", "Network effects both enlarge and protect an early lead, so such markets settle on early winners.", "Difficult",
   ["A loaf of bread is worth no more to its buyer because millions of other loaves are sold the same day.",
    "A telephone is another matter: owned by one person, it is useless, and it grows more useful with every new subscriber who can be called.",
    "Where value grows with the number of users in this way, an early lead tends to feed on itself, as each new user makes the service more attractive to the next.",
    "The same feature that rewards the leader also shields it, since a rival must persuade people to leave the network where their contacts already are.",
    "Because such a lead both grows and protects itself, these markets tend to settle on one or two dominant providers, which need not be the best ones, only the ones that grew large first."],
   "Sentence [[1]] gives the ordinary case of bread. Sentence [[2]] says the telephone is 'another matter', so it must follow the case it contrasts with. Sentence [[3]] generalises from the telephone ('in this way') to the self-reinforcing lead. Sentence [[4]] adds that 'The same feature that rewards the leader' also protects it, which assumes the leader rewarded in [[3]]. Sentence [[5]] concludes from a lead that 'both grows and protects itself', so it needs both the growth of [[3]] and the protection of [[4]].",
   "Opening with the telephone because it introduces the real subject, or placing the conclusion of sentence [[5]] before sentence [[4]], although sentence [[5]]'s claim that the winners 'grew large first' and stay dominant needs both growth and protection.",
   "V01F-network-effects", "The order of the last three sentences depends on the idea: growth of a lead, then its protection, then the market outcome that follows from both; the bread sentence is an unusual, low-key opener.",
   traps=[("21345", "Sentence [[2]] calls the telephone 'another matter', which needs the contrasting case of bread in sentence [[1]] before it."),
          ("12354", "Sentence [[5]] says the lead 'protects itself', but the protection is first explained in sentence [[4]]; placed after the conclusion, sentence [[4]] would merely repeat a point already drawn.")],
   exams=HARD)

pj("V01-F-024", "Science and environment", "An iridium anomaly suggested an impact, and finding the predicted crater made it the leading explanation.", "Difficult",
   ["Geologists studying rock laid down at the time the dinosaurs vanished found a thin band of clay unusually rich in iridium, a metal rare in the earth's crust but common in asteroids.",
    "This suggested that a large asteroid had struck the earth, scattering its dust worldwide, and that the impact might explain the extinction.",
    "If the hypothesis was right, however, so violent an impact should have left a crater of enormous size, of the right age, somewhere on the planet.",
    "The later identification of a buried crater of the predicted size and age, beneath the Yucatan coast of Mexico, turned a bold conjecture into the leading explanation."],
   "Sentence [[1]] reports the observation, the iridium-rich clay. Sentence [[2]] draws the hypothesis from it ('This suggested'). Sentence [[3]] derives a test from the hypothesis: a crater should exist. Sentence [[4]] reports that the predicted crater was found, which only makes sense once the prediction of [[3]] has been made.",
   "Placing sentence [[4]] before sentence [[3]] because the discovery seems to follow the hypothesis directly, although 'the predicted size and age' refers to the prediction set out only in sentence [[3]].",
   "V01F-extinction-impact", "Difficult because the order follows the logic of scientific testing (observation, hypothesis, prediction, confirmation), and the reader must see that the 'however' of the crater sentence marks a test of the hypothesis rather than an objection to it.",
   traps=[("1243", "Sentence [[4]] speaks of 'the predicted size and age', but nothing has been predicted until sentence [[3]] says what the hypothesis requires."),
          ("2134", "Sentence [[2]] begins 'This suggested', which needs the iridium-rich clay of sentence [[1]] as its referent.")],
   exams=HARD)

pj("V01-F-025", "Technology", "Spam filtering is an endless contest because each side's moves teach the other.", "Difficult",
   ["Early filters for junk email worked by blocking messages that contained words commonly found in advertising, such as offers of free money.",
    "Senders quickly learned to misspell those words or hide them in images, so that their messages slipped past the lists.",
    "Filters then began to learn from examples, weighing hundreds of features of each message instead of a few forbidden words.",
    "Each improvement, however, taught senders what the filter was now looking for, and the advantage passed back to them until the next adjustment.",
    "The contest has no final victory for either side, because a filter that learns and an adversary who adapts each supply the other with the lessons it needs."],
   "Sentence [[1]] describes word-list filters. Sentence [[2]] gives the senders' response to 'those words'. Sentence [[3]] gives the filters' counter-move ('then'), learning from examples. Sentence [[4]] says that 'Each improvement', the filters' upgrades just described, in turn taught senders, so the cycle repeats. Sentence [[5]] generalises the cycle into the claim that neither side can win.",
   "Placing sentence [[4]] straight after sentence [[2]], although 'Each improvement' refers to improvements in filters, and the first improvement appears only in sentence [[3]].",
   "V01F-spam-filters", "Difficult because the paragraph is a cycle of move and counter-move; the reader must see that the 'Each improvement' sentence generalises a cycle that the senders' evasion and the learning filters have begun and that the conclusion explains why it never ends.",
   traps=[("12435", "After sentence [[2]], no filter has yet been improved, so 'Each improvement' in sentence [[4]] has nothing to refer to; sentence [[3]] must supply the first improvement."),
          ("12354", "Sentence [[5]] would conclude that the contest never ends before sentence [[4]] has shown that improvements keep handing the advantage back to senders.")],
   exams=HARD)
